# punktfunk host configuration (Windows) — read by the `PunktfunkHost` service.
#
# `punktfunk-host service install` writes a default copy of this to
#   %ProgramData%\punktfunk\host.env
# Edit that file (not this one) and restart the service to apply:
#   punktfunk-host service stop
#   punktfunk-host service start
#
# Format: KEY=VALUE per line; '#' starts a comment. The service loads these into its environment
# and passes PUNKTFUNK_* and RUST_LOG through to the host it launches into the active session.
#
# Most settings are in the web console under Host → Settings. A key the console also offers is
# locked there while it is set here, so use this file to pin a value, not to change one.

# Hardware encode backend. `auto` (default) detects the GPU vendor: NVIDIA->nvenc (direct SDK),
# AMD->amf, Intel->qsv (both native SDKs). Force one with: nvenc | amf | qsv | mf | sw (software
# H.264). `mf` is Media Foundation — any vendor's hardware encoder, 8-bit 4:2:0 only, and the
# automatic second rung when the native backend cannot open.
# nvenc needs the `--features nvenc` build and qsv the `--features qsv` build; amf is always in.
# The published installer carries all three.
PUNKTFUNK_ENCODER=auto

# Video source: `virtual` creates a per-client virtual display at the client's exact resolution +
# refresh — the flagship mode. Requires the bundled pf-vdisplay indirect display driver installed.
PUNKTFUNK_VIDEO_SOURCE=virtual

# A device's own virtual display is captured by IDD-push: straight from the pf-vdisplay driver —
# zero-copy, and it always includes the secure desktop (UAC / lock / login), so the stream survives
# those transitions. Nothing selects it: the former PUNKTFUNK_IDD_PUSH and PUNKTFUNK_SECURE_DDA
# knobs are gone, and a stale line is ignored.

# Stream one of this PC's own monitors to every device instead of a virtual display (the
# console's "Stream this monitor"). The name is the Windows display name. The pointer is part of
# the picture; the lock screen and permission prompts are not.
#PUNKTFUNK_CAPTURE_MONITOR=\\.\DISPLAY1

# The name this host shows up under in Moonlight and the Punktfunk clients. Defaults to the
# machine's computer name; set it to give the box a friendly name without renaming Windows.
#PUNKTFUNK_HOST_NAME=Living Room

# Log level (info | debug | trace). Logs land in %ProgramData%\punktfunk\logs\.
RUST_LOG=info

# The host subcommand the service launches. GameStream (Moonlight) is the console's GameStream
# setting, OFF by default; `serve --gamestream` here would lock it on. `punktfunk-host service
# install` moves such a line, or a missing one, to `serve` with GameStream kept on. A custom value
# you write here by hand is never overwritten by a reinstall/upgrade.
PUNKTFUNK_HOST_CMD=serve

# Multi-GPU boxes only: force the render GPU by adapter Description substring. One pick drives the
# whole pipeline — pf-vdisplay's IddCx render adapter, the IDD-push capture ring, and the encoder
# (NVENC/AMF/QSV) that reads it. A manual GPU preference in the web console outranks this; with
# neither set the host auto-picks the adapter with the most dedicated VRAM. Leave unset on
# single-GPU machines.
#PUNKTFUNK_RENDER_ADAPTER=4090

# Keep a per-client virtual display alive briefly after disconnect so a quick reconnect reuses it
# (no display connect/disconnect chime). Default 10000 ms.
#PUNKTFUNK_MONITOR_LINGER_MS=10000


# Run the virtual display at a MULTIPLE of the session's frame rate, without sending a single extra
# frame, so a frame that just misses the desktop's paint still shows. Unset or 0 is 2 for streams
# up to 500 Hz; 1 turns it off, 4 is the ceiling. A V-Sync game renders at the display's rate
# unless capped. Also on the console's Host → Settings page as Display refresh multiple.
#PUNKTFUNK_VDISPLAY_HZ_MULT=1

# NVIDIA: keep the GPU at full clocks while a session is live. A stream alone leaves the card in
# its idle clocks, where each frame takes two to three times as long to encode (an RTX 4090 at
# 4K60: 11 ms, 5 ms with this on). Costs about 40 W on a high-end card for as long as someone is
# connected, so it is off unless set.
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_CLOCK_BOOST=1

# ---- encoder knobs ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every line below works from THIS file: the host hands them to the pf-vdisplay driver with each
# session's SET_ENCODE, so a change takes effect on the next session. No `setx /M`, no driver
# restart. Unset = the backend's own default. (A machine-wide variable of the same name still
# overrides these inside WUDFHost — a one-box A/B hatch, not a configuration path.)
#
# NVENC split encode across engines: 0 = off, 1 = auto-forced, 2 = two engines, 3 = three.
# Unset picks by pixel rate (4K120 splits, HEVC Main10 below the bar does not).
#PUNKTFUNK_SPLIT_ENCODE=1
# NVENC two-thread retrieve, and its in-flight depth (default 4, clamped 2..=7).
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_ASYNC=1
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_ASYNC_DEPTH=4
# NVENC H.264/HEVC slice count, 1..=32 (default: the session's; 1 = single-slice escape).
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_SLICES=4
# NVENC sub-frame readback: 0 = never, 1 = force (default: the GPU's own cap).
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_SUBFRAME=1
# NVENC concurrent-session budget (default 8, the GeForce limit).
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_MAX_SESSIONS=8
# NVENC live split experiment (1 = arm). Ignored under a forced split or an async session.
#PUNKTFUNK_NVENC_SPLIT_ARBITRATE=1
# Intra refresh: 1 = the periodic wave on AMF/QSV too; 0 = no wave at all, IDR on every loss.
# Unset = the on-demand wave every backend runs where an RFI declines.
#PUNKTFUNK_INTRA_REFRESH=1
# Wave length in frames (>= 2; default half a second of frames).
#PUNKTFUNK_IR_PERIOD_FRAMES=60
# Frames between LTR marks (>= 1; default: the backend's tuning, ~1/2 s AMF, ~1/4 s QSV).
#PUNKTFUNK_LTR_INTERVAL_FRAMES=30
# Spike-only: self-trigger an RFI at this frame to exercise LTR recovery headless.
#PUNKTFUNK_LTR_FORCE_AT=300
# AMF usage preset: ultralowlatency (default), lowlatency, lowlatency_high_quality,
# transcoding, highquality.
#PUNKTFUNK_AMF_USAGE=ultralowlatency
# IDR-only loss recovery on AMF / QSV (1 = disable their LTR).
#PUNKTFUNK_NO_AMF_LTR=1
#PUNKTFUNK_NO_QSV_LTR=1
# HRD/VBV window in frame intervals, one decimal (default 1.0: every frame fits its rate share).
#PUNKTFUNK_VBV_FRAMES=1.5
# PyroWave streamed AUs (1 = arm) and the chunk target in KiB (4..=8192, 64 KiB steps; default 256).
#PUNKTFUNK_PYROWAVE_STREAMED_AU=1
#PUNKTFUNK_PYROWAVE_CHUNK_KIB=256
